{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.NonsingularInverse"],"declarations":13,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.percusp_free_law","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.percusp_free_law","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L188-L194","doc":"The converse reading, which is what the measurement actually reports: with\nthe corrected invariant the constant is `2^{-1/2}` with no `c` in it, and the\n`c = 1` calibration therefore determines every other cusp number. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.percusp_factor_absorbs","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.percusp_factor_absorbs","kind":"theorem","line":164,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L164-L186","doc":"**The per-cusp factor `2^{(c-1)/2}` cancels identically.**\n\nIf the superseded determinant satisfies `|τ_old| = 2^c |τ|` — which holds on the\ndefault triangulations of `4_1`, `L5a1` and `L6a4`, the three cases the factor\nwas fitted to — then\n\n  `2^{(c-1)/2} · |τ_old|^{-1/2}  =  2^{-1/2} · |τ|^{-1/2}`\n\nfor **every** `c`.  So the law carrying a per-cusp factor and the law carrying\nnone are the same function on such data, and agreement at `c = 1, 2, 3`\ndistinguishes nothing.  This is the precise sense in which the \"standard\nper-cusp Gaussian-integration factor\" was a reparametrization: it is exactly the\n`c`-dependence needed to undo the `2^c`.\n\nStated for real `c` and any `t > 0`; the geometric content is `t = |τ|`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.det_sublattice_index","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.det_sublattice_index","kind":"theorem","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L151-L160","doc":"Rows generating a SUBLATTICE of index `k` scale the determinant by `k`.\n\nThis is the failure mode.  An independent subset of the edge rows spans the\nright complex subspace, so it looks correct, but need not generate the lattice;\nthe determinant is then off by the index.  Measured index: exactly `2`, on\n`L5a1`, `L6a4` and `L7a1`, in roughly one randomised triangulation in thirty. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.det_basis_change_unimodular","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.det_basis_change_unimodular","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L142-L149","doc":"A unimodular change of basis leaves the determinant fixed up to sign.  So\nthe 1-loop determinant is well defined — DG's `τ` carries an undetermined sign\nanyway — once the edge rows are a lattice BASIS, e.g. a Hermite normal form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.det_basis_change","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.det_basis_change","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L133-L140","doc":"Changing the integral basis of the row lattice multiplies the determinant by\nthe determinant of the change-of-basis matrix. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.edge_rows_hypotheses_satisfiable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.edge_rows_hypotheses_satisfiable","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L120-L129","doc":"Non-vacuity: the hypotheses of `edge_rows_sum_zero` are simultaneously\nsatisfiable, so the theorem is not vacuously true.  (They are not satisfiable at\n`m = 0` — with no edges the column sums are `0`, not `2` — which is exactly the\ndegenerate reading a vacuity audit should rule out.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.edge_rows_sum_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.edge_rows_sum_zero","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L92-L118","doc":"**The structural fact behind the multi-cusp extension.**\n\nIf every column of the edge matrices sums to `2` — which holds at every cusp\nnumber, because each tetrahedron has six edges in three opposite pairs, one pair\nper shape type, and each is glued into exactly one edge class — then the\nspecialised edge rows sum to zero, hence have rank at most `N - 1`.\n\nNote what is absent: there is no cusp number in this statement, and none in the\nproof.  DG state the column-sum identity for one cusp (\"every edge begins and\nends at the cusp\"), and PR #4708 read that as a hypothesis and concluded the\n`c ≥ 2` extension was blocked.  It is not a hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.nz_pair_reduction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.nz_pair_reduction","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L80-L88","doc":"The specialised row depends only on the Neumann-Zagier pair\n`(a, b) = (G - G'', G' - G'')`.  This is why the relevant lattice lives in\n`ℤ^{2N}` rather than `ℤ^{3N}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.zetaPP_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.zetaPP_eq","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L75-L78","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.zeta_sum_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.zeta_sum_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L66-L73","doc":"The identity that makes the edge rows degenerate: `ζ + ζ' + ζ'' = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.zetaPP","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.zetaPP","kind":"def","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L63-L64","doc":"`ζ'' = 1/(z(z-1))`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.zetaP","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.zetaP","kind":"def","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L60-L61","doc":"`ζ' = 1/(1-z)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.zeta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopEdgeLattice.zeta","kind":"def","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopEdgeLattice.lean#L57-L58","doc":"`ζ = 1/z`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}